PART-02 — ENGINEERING GAP ANALYSIS
1. Purpose

The Engineering Gap Analysis transforms the Repository Reality Assessment into an actionable implementation strategy.

Its purpose is to identify the difference between the current engineering reality and the required Stage-24 engineering target.

This analysis prevents unnecessary implementation while ensuring that every missing institutional capability is identified before coding begins.

2. Mission

Determine precisely what engineering work is required to transition the existing COREI platform into the Stage-24 Institutional Engineering Environment.

The objective is to evolve the platform—not rebuild it.

3. Philosophy

Implementation effort shall be directed only toward verified engineering gaps.

Every proposed implementation shall answer three questions:

Does it already exist?
Is the existing implementation sufficient?
If not, what is the minimum institutional change required?

Engineering shall always prefer evolution over replacement.

4. Gap Analysis Workflow

Every engineering capability follows the same analysis process.

Repository Reality
        ↓
Target State
        ↓
Gap Identification
        ↓
Impact Analysis
        ↓
Implementation Decision
        ↓
Priority Assignment
        ↓
Execution Planning

No implementation begins before gap analysis is complete.

5. Gap Classification

Every engineering capability shall be assigned one institutional gap classification.

G0 — Complete

The capability already satisfies Stage-24 requirements.

No implementation required.

G1 — Enhancement

The capability exists but requires minor enhancement.

Repository remains authoritative.

G2 — Expansion

The capability exists but requires additional institutional functionality.

Implementation extends the existing solution.

G3 — Consolidation

Multiple implementations exist.

One implementation shall become authoritative.

The remaining implementations shall be merged or retired.

G4 — Refactoring

The capability exists but requires structural improvement.

Behavior remains unchanged.

Architecture improves.

G5 — Replacement

The current implementation cannot satisfy institutional requirements.

A controlled replacement is approved.

G6 — New Capability

No implementation exists.

A new institutional capability shall be implemented.

6. Gap Dimensions

Each engineering capability shall be evaluated across multiple dimensions.

Functional Gap

Missing engineering functionality.

Structural Gap

Repository or architectural deficiencies.

Governance Gap

Missing ownership, policies, or standards.

Validation Gap

Missing validation, certification, or quality controls.

Integration Gap

Missing engineering interoperability.

Documentation Gap

Missing institutional engineering knowledge.

Operational Gap

Missing engineering operational capability.

Automation Gap

Missing engineering automation.

Scalability Gap

Implementation cannot support institutional growth.

7. Gap Assessment Matrix

Every engineering capability shall produce an assessment.

The assessment shall include:

current maturity
target maturity
identified gaps
engineering impact
implementation complexity
institutional risk
recommended action
8. Engineering Impact Assessment

Every identified gap shall be evaluated for engineering impact.

Impact categories include:

Critical

Blocks Stage-24 implementation.

Must be resolved immediately.

High

Significantly affects engineering quality.

Resolved before dependent phases begin.

Medium

Improves engineering effectiveness.

Scheduled according to implementation priorities.

Low

Minor improvement.

May be implemented later without affecting Stage-24 progression.

9. Institutional Risk Assessment

Every engineering gap shall be evaluated for institutional risk.

Risk categories include:

architectural risk
operational risk
governance risk
maintainability risk
scalability risk
integration risk
validation risk
organizational risk

Risk informs implementation priority.

10. Engineering Complexity Assessment

Implementation complexity shall be classified.

Level	Description
EC-1	Minimal
EC-2	Low
EC-3	Moderate
EC-4	High
EC-5	Strategic

Complexity is independent of priority.

11. Engineering Priority Matrix

Every engineering gap shall receive one priority.

Priority	Meaning
P0	Mandatory before Phase-01
P1	Required during early Stage-24
P2	Required during Stage-24 implementation
P3	Institutional improvement
P4	Future enhancement

Priority determines execution order.

12. Engineering Decision Matrix

Gap analysis shall produce one implementation decision.

Possible decisions:

Accept Current Implementation
Extend Existing Implementation
Refactor Existing Implementation
Consolidate Existing Implementations
Replace Existing Implementation
Implement New Capability
Defer Implementation

Only one decision becomes authoritative.

13. Dependency Impact Analysis

Every engineering gap shall identify:

upstream dependencies
downstream consumers
implementation prerequisites
validation prerequisites
integration impact

Dependencies determine implementation sequencing.

14. Implementation Effort Assessment

Every implementation task shall estimate:

engineering effort
implementation duration
required expertise
repository impact
validation effort
testing effort
governance effort

These estimates support planning, not scheduling commitments.

15. Gap Resolution Strategy

Engineering gaps shall be resolved according to the following institutional preference:

Accept Existing
        ↓
Extend Existing
        ↓
Refactor Existing
        ↓
Consolidate Existing
        ↓
Replace Existing
        ↓
Create New

Creating new engineering assets is the final option.

